speechtext:
My question is for the Prime Minister, and it concerns the longshoremen's strike. Since all hon. members would like to see this strike settled on the basis of free collective bargaining, and since five weeks have elapsed with no immediate prospects of a solution, may I ask the Prime Minister, first, if he has now any substantial hope of an early settlement;
June 9, 1966	COMMONS
second, if he has not, will he give consideration either to advancing the bill of which the hon. member for Skeena has given notice or introducing a bill which would contain provision for a settlement without imposing compulsory arbitration?
